Permissions.description:
Permissions are queries made to the platform to ascertain which functions the user can access.

The user creates an array of `operations`, each item containing an `action` with an optional `context`

The query follows the format below:

    {
      "operations": [
         {"action":"", "context":{...}},
         {"action":"", "context":{...}},
         {"action":"", "context":{...}}
      ]
    }

Typical usage is either a large list to query or a more precise set of functions, i.e. `CRUD` access to keys.

Query for a large selection of functions:

    {
      "operations": [
        {"action": "ReadUser"},
        {"action": "ReadGroup"},
        {"action": "ReadRecord"},
        {"action": "ReadKey"},
        .....,
        {"action": "ReadBackup"}
      ]
    }

    Note that this order emulates the order of the CipherTrust Manager GUI

Query for specific key functions:

    {
      "operations": [
          {"action": "CreateKey", "context":{"resource":{"meta": { "ownerId": "<USERID>"}}}},
          {"action": "UpdateKey", "context":{"resource":{"meta": { "ownerId": "<USERID>"}}}},
          {"action": "DeleteKey", "context":{"resource":{"meta": { "ownerId": "<USERID>"}}}}  ]
    }

    USERID is the information retrieved from the platform after login, i.e. `local|99b0d0d3-8462-440a-bc6d-7ff0395a5536`

  The response to these queries will be an array of submitted 'function' names, a blank 'context' and an 'allowed' flag (`yes/no/maybe`)

  Response:

     [
       {
         "action": "ReadUser",
         "context": {},
         "allowed": "no"
       },
       {
         "action": "ReadGroup",
         "context": {},
         "allowed": "no"
       },
       {
         "action": "ReadKey",
         "context": {},
         "allowed": "yes"
       },
       .....
       {
         "action": "ReadBackup",
         "context": {},
         "allowed": "no"
       }
     ]

CTE/Clients-GuardPoints.description:
A GuardPoint specifies the list of folders that contains paths to be protected. Access to files and encryption of files under the GuardPoint is controlled by security policies. GuardPoints created on a client group are applied to all clients in the group.

A user can apply guardpoint on specific path on a selected file server. It can be either on a directory, or on a raw device, it can be a applied on a cloud storage, and all these options will be driven by the "guard_point_type" parameter.

After creating a guardpoint user can perform various operations on it. One of them is **delete** operation, which removes the guardpoint configuration from client and server both. Another one is **disable**; which unlike the delete operation, only removes the configuration of guardpoint from agent, whereas its entry will still be available on server for future purposes.

Specific Guardpoints Details:

 Feature | Supported OS | Supported Policies
 --- | --- | ---
 Preserve Sparse Region | Windows, Linux | Live-Data Transformation (LDT)
 Secure Start | Windows | Standard policy and Live-Data Transformation (LDT)
 CIFS | Windows |  Live-Data Transformation (LDT)




Sample for creating GP with *Preserve* *Sparse* *Region* Enabled:

        {
          "guard_paths": [
          <GuardPath_Name>
          ],
          "guard_point_params": {
            "guard_point_type": "directory_auto/directory_manual",
            "policy_id": <policy_name>,
            "preserve_sparse_regions": true/false
          }
        }

          Note: "preserve_sparse_region" value cannot change to True once marked False for a Guardpoint.


Sample for creating GP with *Secure* *Start* Enabled:

        {
          "guard_paths": [
          <GuardPath_Name>
          ],
          "guard_point_params": {
            "guard_point_type": "directory_auto/rawdevice_auto",
            "policy_id": <policy_name>,
            "early_access": true/false
          }
        }


Sample for creating GP with *CIFS* Enabled:

        {
          "guard_paths": [
          <GuardPath_Name>
          ],
          "guard_point_params": {
            "guard_point_type": "directory_auto",
            "policy_id": <policy_name>,
            "cifs_enabled": True/False,
            "network_share_credentials_id":<CIFS connection manager name> (Applicable if 'cifs_enabled' parameter is TRUE)
          }
        }
